No — it's broader than the no-text case. Look at `startChat` (`CourseCreateChat.tsx:176-207`):

```
const startChat = useCallback((text: string) => {
    setStarted(true);
    setComposerValue('');
    if (stagedFiles.length > 0) {
        void chat.startWithMaterials(text, stagedFiles)...
        return;
    }
    if (text) chat.sendMessage(text);
}, ...);
```

The branch into `chat.startWithMaterials(text, stagedFiles)` is gated purely on `stagedFiles.length > 0` — `text` can be anything, empty or not. Two call sites feed `startChat`:

- `handleStartWithMaterials = () => startChat(composerValue.trim())` — the button, only rendered when the composer is empty (`!hasComposerInput`). This is the no-text case.
- `handleWelcomeSend = (text) => startChat(text.trim())` — wired to the composer's own `onSend` (Enter / send icon on `AgentInputWithPlaceholder`). This fires whenever the user submits text, **and it doesn't care whether files are staged or not** — so "drop a PDF, then type 'summarize this' and hit Enter" also routes into `startWithMaterials(text, stagedFiles)` with non-empty text.

So the real trigger condition is "files are staged when the user starts the chat," not "no text was typed." The button is just the *only* affordance for the subset where there's no text (since the composer's own send is inert on empty input).

This doesn't change the fix — my Option A proposal (`send-first`, then upload, then follow-up turn) already handles both uniformly: `startWithMaterials(text, files)` sends `trimmed || "I've added my materials."` as turn 1 regardless of whether `trimmed` is empty, so the "with text + files" case gets the user's real text as the first turn instead of the placeholder, but follows the identical upload-after-`done` sequencing. No branch-specific handling needed.

